### 3a. mTLS refusal probe — NO client cert against the kubelet endpoint

  $ printf 'GET /exec/default/hello-native/hello HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: 127.0.0.1\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n' \
      | openssl s_client -connect 127.0.0.1:10838 -quiet
    Connecting to 127.0.0.1
    depth=0 CN=k3sm-kubelet
    verify error:num=18:self-signed certificate
    ...:error:0A00045C:SSL routines:ssl3_read_bytes:tlsv13 alert certificate required:ssl/record/rec_layer_s3.c:918:SSL alert number 116
    ...:error:0A000197:SSL routines:SSL_shutdown:shutdown while in init:ssl/ssl_lib.c:2804:

  Corroborating probe (LibreSSL curl, independent TLS stack):
  $ curl -k -sS -o /dev/null -w 'http_code=%{http_code}\n' https://127.0.0.1:10838/exec/default/hello-native/hello
    curl: (56) LibreSSL SSL_read: LibreSSL/3.3.6: error:1404C45C:SSL routines:ST_OK:reason(1116), errno 0
    http_code=000

  VERDICT: REFUSED at the TLS layer. "SSL alert number 116" is the TLS 1.3
  certificate_required alert — the exact refusal the PR's gate asserts
  ("no client certificate is refused at the handshake"), now observed live
  against a running dev-boot kubelet endpoint, not only in the unit fake.
  No application-layer response of any kind was returned (http_code=000).

### 3b. kubectl logs / kubectl exec through the apiserver (apiserver holds the client cert)

  $ kubectl logs hello-native
    Error opening /private/var/select/sh: Operation not permitted
    hello from a k3sm native pod
    ProductName:		macOS
    ProductVersion:		26.6.2
    BuildVersion:		25G83

  $ kubectl exec hello-native -- /usr/bin/sw_vers
    ProductName:		macOS
    ProductVersion:		26.6.2
    BuildVersion:		25G83

  VERDICT: PASS. Both `kubectl logs` and `kubectl exec` returned real pod
  output through the mTLS-gated kubelet endpoint — the apiserver's
  CN=k3sm-apiserver-kubelet-client identity was accepted where every other
  probed identity (3a above, and the unit test's foreign-CA / wrong-identity /
  cluster-admin rows) was refused. The "Error opening /private/var/select/sh"
  line is stderr from the pod's OWN `/bin/sh -c` command (a benign macOS shell-
  selection side effect inside the pod's process, captured as part of its
  stdout/stderr stream) — not a kubectl/apiserver/mTLS error, and out of scope
  for this gate.
  The recorded B213 expectation (kubelet SERVING cert not yet signed by the
  cluster CA, breaking logs/exec/attach/port-forward cluster-wide on a
  --mesh-ip cluster) did NOT fire here: this is a single-node dev-boot
  instance with no --mesh-ip, which is exactly the topology B213 does not
  cover — so its absence here is expected, not evidence that B213 is moot.

